  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly Dry/pleasant today and Wed. Highs in the\nupper 70s to the lower 80s both days...albeit a bit more humid \nWed. \n\nA ridge of high pressure in place will result in a beautiful day\nof weather today. A moisture starved northern stream coupled\nwith diurnal heating will result in scattered clouds today, but \nstill expect plenty of sunshine too. While we can not rule out \nthe risk for a brief spot sprinkle/light shower in the\nafternoon, areal coverage and duration would be so limited it is \nnot worth including in the forecast. High temps will mainly be\nin the upper 70s to the lower 80s with comfortable humidity\nlevels too. Low temps tonight should drop into the middle 50s to \nthe lower 60s with light/calm winds along with just some\nscattered to broken mid level cloudiness. \n\nHigh pressure moves off the mid-Atlantic coast by Wed. Highs\nwill again be in the upper 70s to the lower 80s, but it will be\na bit more humid as high pressure moving off the Mid Atlantic\ncoast brings increasing low level moisture. We also do expect a \nbit of a southwest breeze to develop by mid to late afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Breezy, warm, and humid Wed night into Thu with\nbrief scattered showers possible...but the vast majority of \nthis time will be dry. \n\nA cold front will approach the region Wed night into early Thu.\nOut ahead of this front a modest southwest LLJ of 45-50+ knots\nat 950 mb. This should be enough to result in breezy southwest \nwind gusts of 20-30 mph with a few gusts of 35-40 mph possible \nin the vicinity of the upper Cape where they are favored based \non soundings and pattern recognition. \n\nAs for precipitation chances, precipitable water values will \napproach 2 inches ahead of the cold front. However, the low\nlevel moisture is fairly shallow and only deepens in a narrow\nband along the cold front and the better dynamics/forcing is\nacross northern New England. Therefore...only expect a brief\nround of scattered showers with the cold front and the majority \nof Wed night into Thu will feature dry weather. It will be a \nhumid though and the gusty southwest winds will hold low temps \ninto the 65-70 degree range Wed night. Highs on Thu will be warm\nfor this time of year, mainly in the middle 80s but a few spots\nmay reach the upper 80s and it will be humid.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mainly dry/pleasant with lower humidity Fri into\nSat with highs mainly in the 70s. Turning a bit more\nhumid/warmer Sun into Mon.\n\nPost frontal airmass will result in beautiful weather Fri into\nSat with lower humidity. Plenty of sunshine expected both days\nwith highs mainly in the 70s along with comfortable humidity. \nHigh pressure overhead will allow for many locations to fall \ninto the 40s to the lower 50s Fri night/Sat am...which may end \nup being the coolest night for many locations since late last \nspring. \n\nThis high pressure system will shift east of the region by Sun\nand Mon. This will allow for a return southwest flow of a bit \nmore humid/warmer airmass to work back into the region.",
